                        "review": "I have to be honest here. I did not enjoy this game at all.\r\n\r\nI bought To the Moon because I'd heard so much about its emotional story and how it had a way of connecting with players. Unfortunately, for me, the story never gets there. I was never able to connect with what was being told, and the emotional impact that so many people talk about just wasn't there for me.\r\n\r\nFor starters, I found Dr. Eva Rosalene and Dr. Neil Watts quite unlikable. Their banter and behavior with each other often feel childish for a couple of \"doctors,\" and their lack of empathy toward their clients was honestly pretty off-putting. I had a hard time getting invested in the story when the people leading it were difficult to like. Even the way they eventually reach the ending by altering memories didn't really make sense to me, even though I understand what the game was trying to accomplish.\r\n\r\nRiver's reasons for making the paper bunnies was another part of the story that didn't quite work for me. I believe the intention was to show that she wanted John to remember their first meeting, even though he couldn't remember it for reasons beyond his control. I understand what the story was going for, but the way it was portrayed felt like a neurotypical person projecting their own feelings onto someone with Asperger's.\r\n\r\nI've lost a daughter at 21, and I also have a child with Asperger's Syndrome, so loss and autism are both things I have personal experience with. I think this is where my own experience comes into play. How I might feel about a situation isn't necessarily how my kiddo would feel about the same situation. Autistic people don't necessarily experience or express things the same way neurotypical people do, and I know autism is a wide spectrum. My kiddo has never had trouble expressing feelings, but those feelings are often communicated through words rather than the emotional expressions people might expect.\r\n\r\n\"I am sad.\"\r\n\r\n\"I want to cry.\"\r\n\r\n\"That makes me mad.\"\r\n\r\nThose are perfectly clear expressions of emotion, even if they're communicated differently than someone might expect. Because of that, some of the ways River's feelings and motivations were portrayed just didn't land with me.\r\n\r\nI went into To the Moon wanting an emotional story, but I never felt the connection the game was trying to establish. I can understand what it was going for, and I know the story has had a real impact on other players. It just didn't have that effect on me.\r\n\r\nStory aside, the gameplay was pretty rough for me as well. Maybe this was because I played it on the Steam Deck with its smaller screen, but finding memories wasn't intuitive at all. At several points, I found myself clicking all over the screen, trying to figure out what the game actually wanted me to interact with. Instead of feeling immersed in the experience, I was frustrated trying to work out what I was supposed to be doing.\r\n\r\nUltimately, To the Moon just wasn't for me. I wanted an emotional story that would leave an impression on me, but I never connected with the characters or the way the story was told. I understand why others love it, but between the story and the frustrating gameplay, this one left me disappointed.",
